    carePlan: "Start Anastrozole 1mg daily. Refer to radiation oncology. Baseline DXA (done: T-score -1.8). Baseline fasting lipid panel (AIs can increase cholesterol). Schedule oncology follow-up in 3-6 months.",
    dtps: "1. Need for adjuvant endocrine therapy to reduce recurrence risk (~50% risk reduction with 5 years of AI). 2. AI-induced bone loss requires monitoring and bone protection.",
    monitoring: "Annual mammography. DXA every 2 years (or sooner if risk factors). Lipid panel annually. Monitor for AI side effects: arthralgias (50%), hot flashes, vaginal dryness, mood changes. Assess adherence at each visit.",
    counselling: "Anastrozole blocks estrogen production — it reduces the risk of breast cancer returning by about half. The most common side effects are joint pain and hot flashes. Joint pain can be managed with exercise, NSAIDs, or switching to a different AI. Take calcium and Vitamin D daily to protect your bones.",
    followUp: "Oncology clinic in 3-6 months for tolerance assessment. Annual mammography. DXA in 2 years. Consider extending AI to 10 years if high-risk features.",
    pearls: "Aromatase inhibitors (Anastrozole, Letrozole, Exemestane) are the standard of care for postmenopausal women with HR+ breast cancer. They DO NOT work in premenopausal women (ovaries upregulate estrogen production). The SOFT/TEXT trials demonstrated AI superiority over Tamoxifen in postmenopausal women. Oncotype DX recurrence score <18 predicts minimal benefit from chemotherapy. AI-induced arthralgias are common and the most frequent reason for discontinuation — switching to a different AI often helps.",
