    diagnosis: "Chemotherapy-Induced Nausea and Vomiting (CINV) — High Emetic Risk (Cisplatin-Based Regimen) in Locally Advanced Cervical Cancer",
    ddx: ["CINV (high emetic risk)", "Opioid-induced nausea", "Infection-related nausea", "Brain metastases", "Intestinal obstruction"],
    goals: "Achieve complete emetic control (no nausea, no vomiting) during and after chemotherapy, maintain hydration and nutritional status, ensure patient completes full chemoradiation course without treatment delays, improve quality of life",
    pharm: "1. HIGH EMETIC RISK protocol (cisplatin ≥50mg/m2 = high risk; this patient receives 40mg/m2 weekly but cumulative dose and individual sensitivity classify as high risk given her prior severe CINV): Triple antiemetic prophylaxis: a) NK1 Receptor Antagonist: Aprepitant 125mg PO 1 hour before chemo Day 1, then 80mg PO Days 2-3. b) 5-HT3 Receptor Antagonist: Ondansetron 16mg IV (or 24mg PO) 30 minutes before cisplatin — higher dose than standard 8mg due to prior treatment failure. c) Dexamethasone 12mg IV before chemo, then 8mg PO Days 2-4. 2. For cycle 2: ADD Palonosetron 0.25mg IV (long-acting 5-HT3 RA, superior for delayed CINV) instead of or in addition to ondansetron. 3. BREAKTHROUGH nausea (if occurs despite prophylaxis): add Prochlorperazine 10mg PO/IM q6h PRN or Metoclopramide 10mg PO/IV q6h (avoid metocloprazole — extrapyramidal risk with prochlorperazine). 4. Delayed CINV (days 2-5): continue Dexamethasone 8mg PO Days 2-4 + Aprepitant 80mg Days 2-3. 5. If still refractory: consider Olanzapine 10mg daily for 3 days (emerging evidence for refractory CINV). 6. Ensure adequate hydration: IV normal saline 1-2L pre/post cisplatin. Electrolyte replacement: Mg2+ and K+ supplementation.",
    nonPharm: "Small, frequent meals (6-8 per day). Avoid strong-smelling, greasy, spicy foods. Ginger tea (traditional remedy with evidence). Cold or room-temperature foods (less odour). Avoid lying flat for 1 hour after eating. Oral hygiene (rinsing mouth before meals). Acupressure wrist bands (P6 point). Adequate rest before chemotherapy sessions.",
    carePlan: "Pre-medicate: Aprepitant 125mg PO at 10am (1 hour before), Palonosetron 0.25mg IV at 11am, Dexamethasone 12mg IV at 11:30am, Cisplatin 40mg/m2 IV over 1-2 hours. Post-chemo: Ondansetron 8mg IV q8h for 24h, Dexamethasone 8mg PO Days 2-4, Aprepitant 80mg PO Days 2-3. Home antiemetics: Prochlorperazine 10mg PO q6h PRN. IV fluids at home if available (normal saline 1L). Review Day 3 for hydration status and breakthrough nausea. Weight and oral intake assessment.",
    dtps: "1. High emetic risk regimen with prior CINV failure on standard prophylaxis — needs escalated triple antiemetic regimen. 2. Delayed CINV is the most common reason for treatment delays in cervical cancer chemoradiation. 3. Inadequate emetic control leads to dehydration, malnutrition, treatment delays, and reduced survival.",
    monitoring: "Nausea and vomiting assessment (CTCAE grade) before each radiation session. Weight: daily during chemoradiation (any loss >2% in a week requires intervention). Electrolytes (Mg2+, K+, Na+) before each cisplatin cycle (hypomagnesaemia is cumulative). Renal function (cisplatin nephrotoxicity). Hydration status (mucous membranes, skin turgor, urine output). Oral intake diary.",
    counselling: "The most important thing is to take your anti-sickness medicines PREVENTIVELY — before the nausea starts. Once vomiting begins, it is much harder to control. Take the tablets exactly as prescribed, even on days you feel well. If you vomit within 1 hour of taking oral antiemetics, contact us immediately — you may need an injection. Small, frequent meals are better than large meals. Avoid foods with strong smells. Ginger tea may help. Keep a food and nausea diary to help us adjust your medications. Do not skip your radiation because of nausea — we can manage this.",
    followUp: "Oncology Day Unit: before each weekly cisplatin cycle. Radiation oncology: daily during chemoradiation (5 days/week, 5-6 weeks). Dietitian review if weight loss >5%. GP for hydration support between cycles if needed. Psycho-oncology referral for anxiety related to CINV.",
    pearls: "Cisplatin is the most emetogenic chemotherapy agent (emetic risk >90% without prophylaxis). The 3-drug antiemetic regimen (NK1 RA + 5-HT3 RA + Dexamethasone) is the standard of care for high emetogenic chemotherapy. Aprepitant (NK1 RA) blocks substance P in the brainstem vomiting centre and is superior to 5-HT3 RA alone for delayed CINV. Palonosetron has a longer half-life than ondansetron and is more effective for delayed CINV. In Kenya, Aprepitant availability may be limited — when unavailable, high-dose dexamethasone + ondansetron is the fallback. Olanzapine 10mg for 3 days is a low-cost, effective adjunct for refractory CINV. Cervical cancer chemoradiation requires 5 weekly cisplatin cycles — adequate emetic control is essential to prevent treatment delays that compromise outcomes.",
    references: ["NCCN Guidelines: Antiemesis", "ASCO Antiemetic Guidelines", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Oncology Supportive Care", "MASCC/ESMO Antiemetic Guidelines 2023"],
